Patch 0017. The site sells an answer to a question nobody has written down in
public. Anyone deciding whether to pay for a capacity study first wants to
understand the problem, and what they find today is vendor reference designs
written by companies whose every document ends in their own bill of materials.
There is no independent, quantitative account of what actually stops an existing
hall taking AI racks.

So we publish it. All thirteen.

/constraints AND /constraints/<slug>

Each constraint gets a page: what physically runs out, the relation that governs
it, a worked example reproducible by hand, how to tell whether it binds in your
own hall from figures you already have, what relieves it, what that relief costs
beyond money, and the indicative lead time. Cross-linked, with the mistake we
actually see named where there is one.

The numbers are not typed in. Worked examples, residual air loads, floor loadings,
approach temperatures and cost evidence classes are computed from the same
libraries the engine solves with:

  205 A per rack       135 kW / (1.73 x 400 V x 0.95) — a 63 A tap-off feeds a third
  1.6 racks per run    400 A x 0.8 continuous x 400 V, against 135 kW racks
  13.5 kW to air       after 90% liquid capture, at one position designed for 8
  1,944 kg/m2          1,400 kg over 0.72 m2, against a Rated-3 design of ~1,220
  39 K approach        a 45 C platform inlet on a 6 C loop, not the 5 K on the datasheet

A competitor can copy thirteen good pages in a week. Keeping them consistent with a
working solver for two years, while the libraries move and quotations replace
placeholders, is a different undertaking. The test doubles a platform's rack power
and asserts at least four pages move.

/platforms

The platform library published in full — rack power, liquid fraction, residual air,
max inlet, flow, floor loading, feed current, with sources. And the platforms that
are DELIBERATELY ABSENT, with the reason: NVIDIA has not published VR200 NVL144
rack power, ~600 kW for Rubin Ultra is a GTC target rather than a specification,
AMD has not published MI450 rack-level power.

Every competitor's table carries those rows filled with somebody's estimate. The
absences are the most credible thing on the page, and they are the argument for
believing the rows that do have numbers. A generic archetype with no manufacturer
is marked generic rather than published as though somebody built it.

WHAT THE TESTS REFUSE TO LET A PAGE DO

Coverage is asserted in both directions: a constraint added to the engine without a
page fails the build, and so does a page for a constraint that does not exist. No
page may claim a track record (proven, guaranteed, case study, certified), name any
manufacturer of relief equipment, omit a relief lead time, or dangle a cross-link.
The worked examples are recomputed and checked against the engine's own arithmetic.

SEO AND STRUCTURED DATA

The JSON-LD still told search engines this was a practice selling "DC distribution
architecture" and a "physics-informed EMS" — the same stale-company problem the
README had, in the data Google actually reads. Rewritten against what the engine
does, plus a priced OfferCatalog derived from the product table, so what a search
engine is told an engagement costs cannot drift from what checkout charges.

app/sitemap.ts now generates the constraint URLs from the reference file, so a new
constraint is indexed without anyone remembering. TechArticle JSON-LD per page,
canonical URLs, and per-page OpenGraph.

ALSO

  * `gridforge reference [constraints|platforms] [--json -o path]`
  * public/reference/{constraints,platforms}.json, under the existing drift guard
  * the site reads them from disk, not from the engine: these are the most-read
    pages we publish and they must not depend on a service being up
  * every page ends at the free qualifier, which is the point of the whole exercise

TESTS

21 new (331 total). CI gained a reference job step that checks coverage both ways
and scans the published prose for claims we have not earned.

